    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78358.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 0,
    "turn_text": "Well, hello, everyone. And welcome back to a brand new episode of the Eat Local New York podcast. I'm your host, Anthony Tringale. And in this week's episode, my guest is Congressman Brandon Williams. Well, I am thrilled and honored and consider it an incredible privilege to be sitting across from the Congressman and having him here in the studio to have him on the podcast. I'm going to start by saying this. I grew up in a Christian home. I spent half my years in northern Kentucky. I spent half my years here in Syracuse. And growing up in church, growing up in Christianity, especially my family, as we moved, we would visit, we would attend different churches. And so you experienced different leaders. And then I grew up volunteering in church and in my early 20s and working in churches and you experienced different leaders. But whether it's the biblical basis for respect of authority and leadership or it's just common sense. I don't know what it is, but, you know, I grew up just believing that you respect the position, even if you don't respect the person. Now, having said that. Having Congressman Brandon Williams in the podcast today, getting to know a little bit more about him, getting to talk with him, hear his perspective on being in Congress, his life experiences in the Navy, doing just a little bit of research on him, leading up to the podcast. I have great admiration and respect for him as a person outside of the position of a congressman. Listen, if you're going to dedicate any number of years of your life to service for this country in the United States military, you have my respect and admiration, especially as someone who never served. And especially someone who not only served in the US Navy, but then was on a submarine. I mean, a freaking submarine. I could not. That sounds like a nightmare to me to be on a submarine. And so for someone to voluntarily go into that position into that role, I think deserves respect and admiration. And then, you know, just in the role of a congressman and representing our district. Now, why do I start the introduction with that? Well, you know, I posted on my Instagram stories for Eat Local New York. I posted a photo of the Congressman and I side by side when we wrapped the podcast a couple of weeks ago and just was followed up with such a wild amount of hatred in my DMs and ugly things being said to me and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. It's one of the worst things I find about campaigning and politics and just elections in general is how ugly is a society we have become. We have just gone to this disgusting place of attacking the opposite side. I wish that we were more Uh, kind. I wish that we were more accepting. I wish that we were, you know, gentler. I wish we were more understanding as a human race. Um, I'll lump in the entire nation to this just from headline and stuff, but obviously I don't know. I've never, I've never spent time with all of the people in this country to know, um, But even, but it's, you know, honestly, as a person who makes my living off of social media, I blame social media. I mean, I think all of this was built in us to begin with, but you know, social media has given everyone a voice, which I think is the most dangerous thing that could ever happen. because not everybody deserves a voice. And some people have really dumb things to say, and they shouldn't have a platform to say them on. I am included in that group of people. I say stupid things online, but I have the ability to reach too many people online with the stupid things that I say. So having said that, I just the there may be some of those people that are listening to this episode. And if you're listening to this, not that I feel like I'm going to change your mind or change your life by what I'm saying in this introduction, but just to kind of clear the air for anyone who's listening who's semi-interested and why I decided to have the congressman on the podcast. I just wish that we were more accepting of others all around. It's fine to have differing opinions. It's fine to believe one thing that's different than the other than the other group. It's fine if you don't like the fact that I had the congressman on the podcast or if you love that I had the congressman on the podcast. That's okay. You can have those opinions, you can have those thoughts, you can have those feelings. You just don't need to share them. And I'm more than happy to have open conversation with someone who's listening to this who wants to call me and take me out to coffee and have a conversation with me about these things. It's just When we started attacking other people blindly, when we start posting nasty things about people online, I don't know, I think that's just when we draw the line, where I draw the line. So I wanted to have the Congressman on the podcast today because I am fascinated about what it's like to be a Congressman. You know, I got to work a little bit, like a tiny, tiny, tiny, tiny spectrum in politics a few years back when I helped Mark Olson on his first bid for county legislator. And I got to help him with his social media with that. and produce his podcast and help him with some messaging and the photography and videography. And I had an absolute blast. I got to say, one of the most important things that I feel like I've ever done in my life, it really wasn't. On election night with Mark, we were at Seneca Street Brewing and where a lot of the other like Eastern section Republicans were gathered to watch the results pour in. And Mark was like one of the few Republicans that won that cycle, at least for the, you know, Fayetteville manliest group. And Being there with him and with the rest of the group and watching the polls come in and just feeling like it was a victory for myself also was involved in the campaign and being able to sit there and I remember like I had had two different social media post ready to go out. One was unfortunately I lost. One was thank you for electing me and You know, I had those two ready to go and I've never felt more important than when I was when I was coming up with those two like the one of those two posts. It was just the politics, the campaigning politics blog just it's it's exhilarating and I get it. But the level of importance which wasn't real, but the level of importance that I felt just in that small county legislator election, I just, I couldn't imagine what that's like on a national scale. And so, and I can't, and I remember talking with Mark after he was elected and talking with him privately just, you know, what's it like sort of a thing and fascinated about that. And so on a national level, for someone like Brandon to walk into having never been in politics, never held any political or government office prior to this, walking in and becoming a congressman, I'm fascinated at what that's like, how that's changed a person or how it's tried to change someone, you know, how does he feel about his responsibility in this role, all of these sorts of things. All of that said, he and his wife are also farmers here in upstate New York. And of all things, we're trying to cultivate truffles, which I think is even more interesting. And so those are all the reasons why I had the Congressman on the podcast. Uh, having said that, you know, I tried to, my political leanings are not important. I try not to ever share them, uh, publicly because listen, you go into the polling booth and private and you vote and I feel like your view should be kept private and who you side with should be kept private. It's just my opinion. Having said that, you know, when I've done political things in the past with like dinner with the candidates, I try to have the two top candidates or contenders on the show. So I have reached out to John Mannion or his social media account and through his website. sent them a couple Instagram direct messages. I've sent two emails to their campaign through their website asking John to come on the podcast just so I'm not accused of showing biased. They just haven't responded. I don't know if they haven't seen them if they just are interested, whatever the case is. But for all of you listening to this who are pissed off at me for having Brandon, Congressman Brandon Williams in the podcast, I have reached out to John Mannion's office. I just haven't heard back. So that's that. I hope that puts it to rest. I hope if you're listening to this in the beginning, you're like, man, I'm really upset with the local New York and Anthony for having this dirty Republican on the podcast, calm down. I hope that you can understand and appreciate. You don't have to appreciate, but I hope you at least understand where I'm coming from. I respect this position of leadership and authority that Brandon holds. I respect him as a person for serving our country in a pretty significant way while, you know, by being a part of a submarine crew for the United States Navy. And I find him interesting and fascinating. And if you're pissed off for any of those reasons, you don't need to subscribe or follow me or any more. Um, having said that without further ado, let's get into this week's podcast. It's my conversation with a Congressman Brandon Williams. One question is, what is it like, I mean, you don't come from a political background, right? Was anybody in your family ever in politics? No. So you don't come from a political background and then you're a congressman. Not even, you don't even start with like mayor or town council or anything like that. You just go for it. So what is it like? What is that? Is there I mean, what is that shift in your life and your awareness in your mindset and if there is one in who you are going from entrepreneur, farmer, dad, husband to still all of those things, but now Congressman.",
