    "turn_text": "It's a huge shift and nothing really can prepare you for and it's The reason that my wife and I decided to pursue this, and again, we didn't know a single person. We didn't know a single elected official. We didn't know a single person in the party committees. I jokingly say I got tired of throwing things at the TV. And I feel like our nation needs better leaders. And I showed up and I said, I want to run for Congress. I'm not going to lie, I was told that you can't, it's not your turn. And I said, well, my turn started 32 years ago when I volunteered to serve in the Navy, so. There's somebody been sitting around for 32 years, let me know. Otherwise, I think it's my turn. And so we did it all the grassroots way. I mean, you know, Stephanie, my beautiful wife went out and got more than 700 signatures to get on the ballot. You know, typically somebody might get 10 or 15 or 20. She got more than 700. And then we had a lot of volunteers. And then we had a lot more volunteers. And people just liked the authentic message. They liked that I didn't come from politics. But it's a big shift. You know, most days, if somebody walked in and said, hi, Congressman, I would look around and, you know, like, who are they talking to? But at the same time, it is important, you know, the people have hired me to do this job. And I need to show up and make sure that I show up and I fulfill the role of representing them in Congress and, you know, to the best of my ability. We sacrificed everything to do this. I sold my farm equipment and we wrote a $6,000 check to our campaign, which I had to pay ourselves back before the end of the campaign because we had to pay our school taxes. I had to walk away from my software business and a huge ownership in that. Anyway, all of those things made it a great sacrifice, but it's an honor to get to do it. I can't say it's fun. If you want to have fun, I encourage people to do something else, but it's an honor. I think these are unusual times in our history. We're here to serve.",
