    "turn_text": "toward it? Very much the same way. It is, you know, I think about, you know, people that are listening to this, you know, that are in, potentially in the food industry, that are in the hospitality industry. that are in agriculture, maybe farm to table. Once you learn to follow your passion and really identify where your giftedness is and what gets you up in the morning. Um, the earlier you learned that the better, particularly if you, you learn a craft, if you really learn how to deliver value and make, you know, a living off of it. And, um, so one of the things that I've discovered after career and business now, of course, I started my career in military service and, uh, it's very important to me. I was not a, uh, you know, I didn't go to the service academies. I didn't have a ROTC scholarship and I was a volunteer and, um, That decision came after I'd been in China and had been witness to the student protests in Tiananmen Square in May of 1989. And then I spent the next year as a visiting student at Harvard University for Asian Studies and Chinese Studies. And it was in the middle of that first semester, there were a number of the student leaders had fled. This was, of course, after the crackdown. And they had fled to Harvard. And one of them was my tutor. And there was something that stirred in me that just I decided I was going to volunteer to serve in the Navy and sought out the nuclear submarine program. And that was a great decision for me. I wish I would have listened a little bit more deeply at the time because that sense of duty is a very strong motivator for me and also a sense of utility being useful. And I certainly got that in my military service now. I was very fortunate to meet Stephanie when I was a young naval officer. And in fact, I met her at a, you mentioned you worked at a church. I met her at a Bible study. And I proposed eight weeks later. So I always tell people, keep your daughters away from sailors. That's the important lesson there.",
