  {
    "episode_id": 78358,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78358.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I just had somebody who sent me a DM on Instagram maybe three or four weeks ago and he said, I'm currently a teacher and I forget where he was somewhere in western New York. So I'm currently a teacher, been a teacher for like 10 years, obsessed with the restaurant industry, really went open up my own food truck to start and then get my own restaurant. I'm curious, what advice can you give me on starting a food truck and documenting it?\" And so I said, well, advice on starting a food truck or advice on starting a food truck and documenting. He said, let's start with advice on starting a food truck. And I thought about it for a couple of days and then I wrote back and I said, don't do it unless it's the only thing that you can possibly think about. You can't sleep at night because you're just constantly thinking about the food you make and what your menu is going to be and the name of it and how you're going to be successful. And if it's the only thing that consumes you. then you shouldn't start a food truck. Because talking to so many restaurant owners, food truck owners, people who get into the food service, business, brewery, whatever the case is, it is such an incredible, like you said, grind. It's so challenging, especially nowadays, especially here in Central New York. It's incredibly difficult. Um, and people that I know that it could be their first year could be their 20th year. They're still in a very similar position of trying to figure out a way to make it work. And that's actually, you know, it's a lot of business owners that I've talked to, not just in hospitality. It can be an incredibly lonely, lonely, lonely space. If you, especially if you don't have some sort of a support or friend group that's in the same similar position as you are, other restaurant owners or business owners, it can be very, very challenging. At something I've learned, I kind of always felt like with my business, I was like kind of had it easy on the outside of that a little bit, but even now as I've progressed, I've had this now for eight years and full time for three, I'm kind of seeing that also. It can be very challenging in the food because it's so up and down. Now, traveling around the state, I definitely see that in all different areas, but it feels like it is a little bit more concentrated here in Central New York than it is. And I go out to Rochester or Buffalo, and not to say that it's any easier there, but it is a very different mindset, even just an hour to the West in the restaurant industry than it is here in Central New York. And I don't know why that is. Um, you going into Congress, what was that like in the very beginning? Was it, was it, did it feel very lonely? Was there, were there, were there, you know, other senior, you know, congressmen and women that were there to kind of welcome you and, you know, hey, here's how it works. And we go here on Tuesday and this is what's expected of you. Is there a. Was there a training manual that you got walked through or was it just here's the keys to the office and we'll see it roll call?",
