    "turn_text": "It was somewhere in between. You know, it's a little bit like how much truth do you want here? It is they do make a good effort to prepare you with the basics because you know you're standing up you know, multiple offices, right? I have an office in Utica via office in Syracuse. And then of course, my Washington DC office and you're standing up a staff. So, you know, you're hiring 12 to 15 to 16 people and that are expected to fulfill a role for you, right? That's how you're going to, you know, meet the constituent services and service them. That's how you're going to run your legislative agenda. That's how you're going to you know, schedule your day, which is a very unenviable task. And so it definitely took some orientation. And they put together, you know, kind of, you know, preschool for freshman congressmen that you go through. And after the election, there's a couple of weeks, and it's very helpful. But back to the truth, politics can be a very lonely business in the sense that everyone's looking out for themselves. And it's very transactional. It's not nearly as collegial or relational as you would think. And so that was a bit of a reorientation, and a lot of people have previous connections and networks in politics, and I had none. But for me as an outsider, that was a good way. I wouldn't have it any other way, because I got to be myself and figure out my own way. You know, one of the key lessons is that you need to have good people around you, and that took some time, you know, to figure out, you know, who can you trust, who's really helping you succeed. And then I came up with a framework, again, I'm a nuclear engineer, so I'm always looking for, you know, structure, reason, repeatable, you know, sort of processes.",
