  {
    "episode_id": 78358,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78358.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "And I drew five circles all around, like five points on a star. And at the top, I put my life. That's my marriage. That's my health. That's my sanity. And that's one of the main things I have to manage. The second is being an effective member. So as a member of Congress, you are individually responsible for a lot. You're responsible for your relationship with your colleagues, you're responsible for your brand, you're responsible for what committees you're on. I, as a congressman, I have my own, almost like a little business. And then the next one is an effective office. And that is constituent services. That is when people come to DC that they can get a tour. That is the administration of three different offices and a staff of 15 or so. So you have an administrative managerial function because your office has to work well. The part that nobody likes, at least nobody that I know, is a fourth circle, which is fundraising, particularly in a competitive seat like mine. It feels like a full-time job, and sometimes, some days it is. But that's a reality of modern politics. Everybody hates money in politics, but there is money in politics. Fundraising is a major part of the job. And then the last one is the campaign. I'm on a two-year cycle, and so I'm up for reelection all the time. You have to have a campaign staff, by the way, which is completely separate, and you run a campaign organization. So those are the five circles, and they orbit around whatever your central mission is. And, you know, my mission statement that I said to my team very early on is to love and serve the people of Central New York. And so all five of those circles are to support that mission. And, again, if I'm truthful, there's a very large number of people in politics who's the center of their mission is to raise money, get on TV and get reelected",
