    "turn_text": "I would typically, when I was thinking about I was thinking about your position in politics and government and that connection to central New York to your district and its people and I was trying to think of like, well, how does that really play a role? I remember talking to Mayor Ben Walsh like four, six years ago, I think it was his first term running and I asked him because I remember when former mayor Stephanie Miner was in office there were things and you know it's so it's so easy for somebody like me to sit here and like see news clips read headlines have our own interpretation of what's happening in government or politics and then really at the end of the day I have zero clue what's actually happening but I just remember Stephanie mayor Stephanie Miner she was it was something about Syracuse with a refugee city whatever the heck it was called Um, and that got national attention. I remember going into moving back, going into the next race, I was thinking, man, I don't want to see my mayor of the city I live in on Fox or CNN, like, you know, stay in your lane. And so I remember asking, you know, mayor Walsh about that and. You know, his response was, my job is to make sure trash is picked up on time. There's firefighters when they're needed police officers. Like that's my goal. You know, that's my job and I have to do that properly and I have to be focused on that. And that's it. And I love that response. You know, I still think about that response all the time and thinking about jobs and what is my real job here. But when I think about your position, I think almost it's how much of effect do you have on the day-to-day life of people in the district? Or is it more of maybe there's a terrible example, but you're more of the Iron Dome, so to speak, for the district in Washington, trying to make sure there aren't major things",
