  {
    "episode_id": 78358,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78358.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "The more you care about our country, the harder the job is of being in elected office. If you actually care, it tends to take it more personally. But the other side of it is, I'm okay with, I don't really pay attention to the criticism. And I try to listen to the people that I'll tell you a story. I was visiting one of the residential high rises for the subsidized housing in Syracuse. And a woman came up to me and clearly distraught and she said that her son had died in prison and that she was unable to get information about his death. And even though she had tried and she got some kind of cursory remarks and And she told me a bit of his background and the struggles that he'd had and her granddaughter, his daughter that was now being cared for in her family. And I was able to go get some insight and some specific details about what had happened. I came back several weeks later and was able to sit down with her in private and walk through these very difficult things. They were emotional things for her, traumatic things to hear some specifics about her son's passing. But it's one of the most, maybe it's one of the most satisfying things I've gotten to do in Congress because you can use the power of the office to really bless someone. And I've had a few opportunities to make a difference of someone traveling and is in crisis and they lost their passport and they have my cell number. I've gotten the embassy on the phone at 4 AM and they'll take my call.",
