    "turn_text": "then, so anyway, that's what it sounded like when it was on paper. And we planted 2,350 hazelnut trees ourselves. And simultaneously, we planted 10,000 lavender plants. And Then, pretty soon Stephanie had 30 plus beehives. While we were waiting for the hazelnut trees to develop, and these were hazelnuts that had been inoculated in the fungi spores of burgundy travels, our lavender and particularly our honey business really took off. for Stephanie and it was agritourism. Stephanie was on the Kyuga County Tourism Board. We have busloads of people that would come and she would teach them about bees. They would shop at our store and it was an hour and a half and we could make a lot of money in an hour and a half. So, the agritourism kind of small farms approach is what we have found rewarding. And, you know, Congress in life gets in the way. Stephanie's mom, you know, for the last year of her life lived with us for most of that and had broken her hip and needed, you know, very intensive care that Stephanie could provide. There's things and any farmer will tell you there's that life gets in the way and and it's hard so I would say that we've learned a lot I think we've been you know blessed to be part of the farming community We did a CSA community supported agriculture during covid because",
