    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78358.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. I remember at Abbott Farms, you know, that actually the similarities between Heritage Hill and Abbott's are very, very odd because both Dan and Warren Abbott were off the farm, not involved in the farm business, and not involved in the family farm. I believe both were in New York, both in finance, and then got called back to help take over the farm. When I was with the Abbots, it was lots of kids and none of them wanted to have anything to do with taking over the farm. And you know, Abbots' apples and blueberries and pumpkins and all sorts of stuff. And a ton of property out in Baldwin'sville. That they've had to sell parcels off for whatever to whomever to keep the farm going throughout the years. But I would sometimes leave that farm at midnight and see Warren across the street with a headlamp on. rebuilding the strawberry fields on his hands and knees or I would see his dad at 90 years old or 88, whatever it was, in the tractor, going through the apple trees. The amount of work that about three people put into that farm was sobering to see. Hospitality is always a very difficult business, and especially when you add in the farm element that is the main economic driver for your family. And you don't have the people, you don't have the hands, the laborers to help you out with it. That just means you're up for 18 hours a day, sometimes working, farming to make sure everything's taken care of. That was pretty sobering to see. I",
